WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
Ban the box-laws widened the black-white job callback gap from 7 to 43% in New Jersey and New York City. This is unintended consequences, episode 152. This is the hope that hid the record, the staggered rollout that made it test possible. The substitution employers made once the question was gone, the later rules layered on top, and the trait a designer still has to name. In 2018, the quarterly Journal of Economics published a field experiment built for that exact change.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:35.240
Researchers sent roughly 15,000 fictitious job applications in New Jersey and New York

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:39.180
City before local banned the box rules took effect and after.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.960
The applications were not a survey of opinions.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.820
They were an attempt to watch what a real employer did with a real inbox once a familiar

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:47.900
question disappeared.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.860
Criminal records had cut callbacks sharply at employers that still asked the question.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.260
The policy had been written to give people with convictions a fairer first look.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.720
Instead, it enlarged the racial disparity that remained after the record was hidden.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:07.200
Amanda Aggan and Sonya Star designed the correspondence study so the applications could be compared

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:07.800
cleanly.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.180
The fictitious applicants were young men.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.560
Their names signaled race, education, experience, and address were otherwise identical.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.480
The only variable that changed systematically after the policy was the absence of the

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.400
conviction question.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:26.520
That design matters because a widening gap in callbacks could not be laid on differences

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.800
in applicant quality. The paper showed the effect concentrated among employers that had previously

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.660
asked the question. Those were the firms whose information environment actually changed.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.800
Because the study tracked the same set of employers across the policy change,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.340
it ruled out a simpler story. Firms newly entering the market or leaving it were not what drove

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:52.420
the result. Hold the labor market constant, hold the employer constant, remove one line from the form,

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.600
and the callback pattern still moved.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.200
The researchers read that movement as statistical discrimination.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.020
Without the record, some employers used race as a proxy for the likelihood of a criminal

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:03.520
history.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:09.220
A separate analysis by Jennifer Dollyak and Benjamin Hanson looked past the callback

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.560
and into employment itself.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:17.740
Using current population survey data from 2004 to 2014, they found ban the box policies

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:23.420
reduced employment probabilities by 3.4 percentage points for young black men without college degrees.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:29.080
The same analysis found a drop of 2.3 percentage points for young Hispanic men in that group.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:35.860
Those drops were largest precisely where criminal record prevalence differs most across racial lines and national data.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.840
That group is young men without college degrees.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:45.660
The pattern is consistent with employers updating their beliefs about the probability that an applicant in that group has a record.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.420
once the direct information is withheld. None of that was the future the first

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.220
legislators were trying to build. The first ban the box law appeared in Hawaii in

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.440
1998. Legislators there wanted to reduce the automatic exclusion of applicants

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:05.100
who had served their sentences and could not get past the initial form. Picture the

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.460
moment they were trying to interrupt. A person has finished a sentence. A job is

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.880
open. The application arrives and a checkbox ends the conversation before a

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.880
human being has heard a word about the work.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.780
That is a hard problem to look at and do nothing.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.020
People returning from prison faced unemployment rates

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.700
several times the national average.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.760
Lawmakers connected that fact to a second risk.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.940
Lack of steady work raised the chance

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.320
that someone would reoffend,

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.840
not because a check box predicted character,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:34.840
but because idleness and poverty

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.680
make a stable life harder to hold.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.960
The reform was aimed at that chain.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.080
Get the person to a conversation.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:42.980
Let the record be explained.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:47.220
give work a chance to do what punishment already finished had not done alone.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.040
By the mid-2010s, more than 30 states in the District of Columbia had passed

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:56.820
versions of the idea. Some versions applied to public employers, some reached

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.740
government contractors, some covered all private firms. The details differed,

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.400
and that difference will matter in a moment. But the shared bet was the same.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:09.340
Hide the conviction at the front of the process, and a fairer hearing becomes

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.940
more likely. In 2015, the federal government removed the question from its own job applications.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:20.600
Proponents reasoned from a simple sequence. If employers never saw the conviction until an interview

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.440
had already begun, qualified candidates would have a chance to explain their record in person.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:31.120
They would not be filtered out by a checkbox. The logic rested on an observation that many people

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.660
who had hired or tried to get hired already recognized. Many employers used the box as a quick

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.040
screen even when the offense bore no relation to the job duties.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.620
A conviction for something unrelated to the work could function like a closed door.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.980
Time is scarce in a hiring office, a box is fast, a conversation is slow.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:54.980
The reformers were not imagining cruel people so much as busy people using the cheapest

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:56.060
signal on the page.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.720
Their answer was to take that cheap signal away, at least until a person was already

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.220
in the room.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.720
It is worth sitting with how reasonable that answer was on the information they had.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.440
They could see the screen, they could see the unemployment, they could see the path from

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.140
joblessness back toward harm.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.100
What they could not yet see in 1998, or even in the early wave of state laws, was the

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:21.020
signal an employer would reach for once the box was gone.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.340
Cities and states adopted the rules at different times.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.960
That stagger created natural experiments.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.660
New Jersey and New York City both changed their requirements in the years covered by the

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.880
again in star study. An employer in one place could be observed before the rule and after

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.040
it, while the surrounding labor market was held as constant as a real city allows. That

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.540
is why a field experiment could isolate the effect of removing the box rather than

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:51.740
blaming the difference on a boom or a slump. Early reports from advocates noted something

00:05:51.740 --> 00:06:01.640
the reformers had hoped to see. More applicants with records reached the interview stage. On

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:06.220
for some people with convictions the wall moved, supporters pointed to the growing number

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:11.120
of jurisdictions that had acted. They also pointed to the Obama administration's decision

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:16.360
as evidence that the approach was workable and humane, a policy that starts in one state

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:22.620
spreads past 30 and reaches federal applications looks from the inside like a success gathering

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.560
proof. Few contemporaneous accounts examine the other half of the employer's problem.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:32.280
If the conviction question is gone, what does a risk averse hire or use instead?

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.960
The advocates were watching the applicants the law was written for.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:41.180
The applicants without records, whose name still signaled race, were easier to leave out

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:42.560
of that first round of stories.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.180
Both things can be true in the same season.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:50.440
More people with records can reach an interview, and employers who used to sort on a verified

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.380
fact can start sorting on a proxy.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.640
The staggered map made that second movement measurable.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.800
It did not, at the time, make it obvious to the people passing the laws.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:05.040
Before the laws, the employers that used the box were not ignoring race, and they were

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.420
not ignoring records either.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.540
Those employers called back white applicants with records at lower rates than black applicants

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:12.280
without records.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.880
Read that slowly, because it is the world the check box was actually producing.

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:21.720
A record could outweigh the advantage that whiteness otherwise carried in that callback

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:22.080
pool.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.960
The box was a blunt instrument, and it was also a piece of information that cut across

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:27.920
the racial signal.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:33.260
After the box was removed, the black-white callback gap among applicants without records

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.560
widened dramatically at those same employers.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.460
These were not different firms with different cultures.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:42.300
These were the firms that had asked, and then were told not to ask.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:47.360
The information left on the application was what a name, a school, an address, and a work

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.580
history could still convey.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:53.100
day. Race remained visible in the way the experiment constructed it, through the name.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:59.760
The conviction did not. Statistical discrimination is a cold phrase for a human guess. An employer

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.380
wants to avoid a hire they believe is more likely to carry a record, and they no longer

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:08.500
have the record. They reach for the group trait that, in the data they carry in their

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:13.660
head, correlates with that record. Young black men, in the national pattern the later

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:18.360
employment study leaned on, sit in the demographic where that correlation is widest.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:21.680
The guest does not require the employer to announce a theory.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:26.480
It requires only that the callback button get pressed less often when the name fits the

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:26.920
proxy.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.460
The experiment's young men without records absorb that guess.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:34.500
Their applications matched on education, experience, and address.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:39.220
What differed was the name and the fact that the conviction question was no longer there

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:40.280
to contradict the guess.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.140
Second order, the people the law meant to lift were not the only people in the file.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:48.920
Applicants with records could gain a first look they had been denied.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.920
Applicants without records, if they belonged to the group employers treated as higher risk,

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:59.160
could lose callbacks they would have received when the box led an employer see a clean history directly.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.560
Third order, the employment numbers moved in the same direction as that guess.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:07.200
Dollyak and Hanson were not counting fictitious emails.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.020
They were looking at whether young men without college degrees were working, in the years

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:13.720
and places the policies arrived.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:18.180
A callback gap is an insult you can measure in a lab-like field test.

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:23.760
An employment drop is rent, transit, and the next application paid in the actual labor

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:24.180
market.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:29.520
The researcher's interpretation ties those drops to the withheld fact, where record

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:35.020
prevalence differs most by race, hiding the record gives race more work to do as a stand-in.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:39.680
That is not a claim that every employer did this, or that every jurisdiction saw the

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:40.720
same size of effect.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.240
It is a claim that the average pattern in the current population survey window from 2004

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:50.980
to 2014 moved against the groups the proxy would be expected to burden.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:55.860
And it is a claim that the quarterly journal of economics experiment locates the change

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:59.900
in the firms that lost the question, not in the firms that never asked it.

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:04.320
The policy changed the information environment in which hiring decisions were made.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:06.520
Employers did not stop deciding.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:09.760
They decided with a poor file and a remaining signal.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:15.340
Sanja's star told Jezebel in 2016 that the findings were not necessarily an indictment

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:16.040
of the policy.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:21.340
The reason she gave is worth keeping, because it refuses a simple victory lap in either

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:21.820
direction.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:25.160
Removing the box still helps some applicants with records.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.240
Addressing racial bias directly remained difficult.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:33.220
A result can be real, and still not tell a legislature to pretend the original problem

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:33.880
has vanished.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.140
No large-scale repeal followed.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.080
The checkbox did not sweep back across the map.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:44.140
Some jurisdictions later added requirements that employers consider the relevance and age

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:44.780
of convictions.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.440
Some require that the question wait until a conditional offer.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:52.560
Those additions are an attempt to keep the humane aim and narrow the harm.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:56.780
Relevance is a sensible word if the offense has nothing to do with the job.

00:10:56.780 --> 00:11:01.560
age of the conviction is a sensible word if a person is being judged forever for a

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:05.640
finished sentence. A delay until a conditional offer tries to preserve the

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:11.060
interview the Hawaii legislators wanted in 1998. The core mechanism though remains

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:15.900
in place in dozens of cities and states. Hiding one verifiable fact still leaves

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:20.240
employers to rely on other signals. Later versions of the policy layered

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.880
additional rules on top of the original disclosure change rather than

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:29.420
restoring the box. That choice preserved the information asymmetry. It tried at the same

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:34.340
time to constrain how employers could act on the remaining signals. Whether those constraints

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:39.660
bind as a harder thing to see from a statute than from a call-back log, a rule can tell

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:44.700
an employer to weigh relevance. A rule cannot by itself stop a tired reader from letting

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.120
a name do the work a missing line used to do.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:52.980
The aftermath is not a clean reversal and not a clean vindication. It is a policy that

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:57.660
still helps some of the people it named, still shifts burden onto people it did not mean

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:01.500
to burden, and still governs hiring forms in a great many places.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:06.600
When a disclosure rule withholds one fact, the next decision does not become neutral.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:12.060
It becomes a search for the next most available signal, and in this case that signal was race,

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:15.500
read off a name in a pool of otherwise identically young men.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.700
The substitution showed up among the employers who had used the box, which is the footprint

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:26.020
you would expect if the policy itself changed the file, not if the economy merely changed clothes.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:31.200
The same substitution showed up again as lower employment probabilities for young black men

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:36.640
without college degrees, and for young Hispanic men in that group in the years the policy spread.

00:12:37.513 --> 00:12:42.153
A designer who wants to know whether a disclosure rule is working cannot stop at the applicants

00:12:42.153 --> 00:12:47.233
the rule was written to release from a screen. The data that later judged the rule are the same

00:12:47.233 --> 00:12:49.353
data that can show who absorbed the proxy.

00:12:50.013 --> 00:12:54.473
Track the group whose observable trait now stands in for the hidden fact and track them

00:12:54.473 --> 00:12:57.533
in the same places before and after the form changes.

00:12:57.933 --> 00:13:02.913
If you follow how a missing fact gets priced into a decision, the way a missing line on

00:13:02.913 --> 00:13:08.413
a hiring form got priced into a callback, modern investing techniques is the Narin Network

00:13:08.413 --> 00:13:13.013
show that stays with substitution when the stakes are money rather than a job application.

00:13:13.533 --> 00:13:18.513
A rental platform or a lender can drop one hard question from the first screen and still

00:13:18.513 --> 00:13:21.933
leave a name, a neighborhood, and a school in plain sight.

00:13:22.513 --> 00:13:27.213
If that field disappears this year, what trade is left on the form, and whose approval

00:13:27.213 --> 00:13:29.093
rate would you watch in the quarter after?

00:13:29.713 --> 00:13:32.953
We'll leave it there for today, thanks for thinking through this one with me.

00:13:33.433 --> 00:13:37.913
If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way, and I'll see you

00:13:37.913 --> 00:13:38.793
tomorrow with another.

